-- title:
--   Ramification away from p forces q to divide the newform level
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a complete discrete valuation ring that is a domain of characteristic zero with finite residue field, $p$ a prime with $p$ lying in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal O$, and let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal O$, i.e. a two-dimensional $k$-vector space $V$ together with a monoid homomorphism $\bar\rho.\rho$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)=\overline{\mathbb Q}\simeq_{\mathbb Q}\overline{\mathbb Q}$ to $\mathrm{End}_k V$ factoring through a finite level, assumed absolutely irreducible (irreducible after base change to $\overline k$). Let $S$ be a finite set of primes containing $p$, and $N\ne 0$ with every prime factor of $N$ in $S$, with level $N$ weight $2$ possessing an integral structure (the integral lattice spans the cusp forms over $\mathbb C$). Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra $\mathrm{heckeAlgebra}\,N\,2\,S$ (the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$ generated by the $T_\ell$ with $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S$, and the $U_q$ with $q\mid N$, $q\notin S$) to $k$, such that for every prime $\ell\nmid N$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in which $\ell$ is a non-unit and every $\sigma$ lying in the decomposition group of $P$ and acting as $x\mapsto x^\ell$ on the residue field, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho.\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\theta(T_\ell)X+\ell$. Let $\mathcal O'$ be a second such complete discrete valuation ring, finite over $\mathcal O$ by a local algebra map, and $\psi$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map from $\mathrm{heckeLocal}\,N\,S\,\mathcal O\,\theta$ (the localisation of the Hecke base algebra at the complement of the prime attached to $\theta$) to $\mathcal O'$. Let $M\ne0$ divide $N$, let $g$ be a weight-two cusp form on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform in the project's sense (a normalised eigenform whose good eigensystem occurs at no proper divisor of $M$), let $\mathrm{chig}$ be a ring homomorphism from $\mathrm{heckeAlgebra}\,M\,2\,S$ to $\mathbb C$ sending $T_\ell$ to the $\ell$-th $q$-expansion coefficient of $g$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$ outside $S$, and let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the range of $\mathrm{chig}$ to $\mathcal O'$ matching $\psi\circ\pi$ on the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\nmid N$ outside $S$. Then every prime $q\ne p$ at which $\bar\rho$ is ramified (that is, $\bar\rho.\rho$ is not identically $1$ on the inertia subgroup of some valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in which $q$ is a non-unit) divides $M$. The hypothesis that all members of $S$ are prime is also assumed.
--
--   This is the level-lowering bookkeeping step saying that the level of a newform realising a point of the local Hecke algebra is divisible by all primes $q\neq p$ of ramification of the residual representation; equivalently, $\bar\rho$ unramified at $q$ is forced whenever $q\nmid M$. It is used in the analysis of the $U_q$-eigenvalues and in the identification of points of the local Hecke algebra with $q$-expansion coefficients of newforms.
